## What it is

A type can decide how it prints. Define `fn to_text(self: T, spec: text) -> text` and `"{x}"` calls it; `"{x:anything}"` hands `anything` straight through, so the type owns its whole format vocabulary.

## How it aids you

Money, dates, colours and units print the way they should everywhere, once, instead of at every call site. And because the spec string is passed through untouched, the language never has to learn a date or currency vocabulary — your type defines what its own specs mean.

## Example

```loft
value struct Money { cents: integer }

fn to_text(self: Money, spec: text) -> text {
    whole = self.cents / 100;
    rest = self.cents % 100;
    out = if spec == "raw" { "{self.cents}" } else { "{whole}.{rest:02}" };
    out
}

fn main() {
    m = Money { cents: 1250 };
    println("{m}");        // 12.50
    println("{m:raw}");    // 1250
}
```

The companion hook is `fn OpConvTFromS(v: S) -> T`, which makes `s as T` work — together they let a library type behave like a built-in.

## What it is

`type_of(x)` hands a program the declared shape of a type as ordinary data — its name, the bytes one record occupies, and every field with its own name, type name and byte offset. An enum reports its variants; a vector or keyed collection names its element type. When the name only arrives at runtime — from a config file, a database catalogue, a command line — `type_named("Row")` asks the same question with no value in hand, and answers `null` when this program has no such type.

`field_value(x, position)` is the other half: what a VALUE holds at one of those positions. `type_of` says a record has a `text` at byte 16; this reads it. A field of an **inline struct** is reached with a PATH instead — `field_value(doc, [8, 0])` is `doc.origin.x` — because a nested record reports its fields relative to itself, so one number cannot name a field inside one.

## How it aids you

A generic serialiser, an ORM mapping or a schema check can be written once, against any type, instead of once per type by hand. The answer is read from the same layout descriptor the store and the browser bridge already use, so reflection cannot drift into a second, disagreeing description of the same memory — and because both halves read that one descriptor, a position `type_of` hands out is a position `field_value` can read.

## Example

```loft
struct Point { x: integer, y: integer }
struct Row { id: integer, origin: Point, name: text? }

fn main() {
    row = Row { id: 7, origin: Point { x: 3, y: 4 }, name: null };

    t = type_of(row);                       // the TYPE of row, not its value
    println("{t.name} — {t.size} bytes");
    for f in t.fields {
        // ... and what THIS row holds there. `null` is not `""`.
        v = field_value(row, f.position);
        shown = if v.is_null { "null" } else if v.kind == TextKind { v.t }
                else if v.kind == IntegerKind { "{v.i}" } else { "(descend)" };
        println("  {f.name}: {f.type_name} @{f.position} = {shown}");
    }

    // An inline struct holds no value of its own — reach INTO it with a PATH.
    // [8, 0] is `row.origin.x`: the offsets are walked one at a time, never added.
    println("  origin.x = {field_value(row, [8, 0]).i}");

    // The name can also arrive at runtime — from a config file or a catalogue.
    u = type_named("Row");
    if u == null { println("no such type") } else { println("found {u.name}") }
}
```

`type_of` reads its argument for its TYPE and never evaluates it — the contract C's `sizeof` has — so pass a variable, a field or a parameter rather than an expression with a side effect. `field_value` does evaluate its argument: the value is what it reads.

**A position is checked against the type's own descriptor, never trusted.** Only a field the type says BEGINS there is read; a hand-made offset, a stale one, one belonging to a different type, or the interior of a wider field all answer `OtherKind` with no read at all. And `kind` is the descriptor's answer rather than the caller's — passing the position of an `integer` field does not make the reading an integer, it makes it whatever that field is.

**A path is walked, never summed**, and that check is what keeps this from being a pointer: every step must begin a field of the type it is read against, and `origin`'s offset plus `x`'s begins nothing in the owner. Every step but the last must land on an inline record — a step onto a scalar, a vector, a keyed collection or a stored reference answers `OtherKind` and reads nothing, since following a stored reference would be a pointer chase rather than a field read. A one-element path answers exactly what the bare position does, and an empty path answers `OtherKind`.

`ValueInfo` keeps three answers apart, because an ORM that confused them would write the wrong row: `OtherKind` (nothing begins there), a non-scalar kind (a nested record, a vector, a keyed collection — nothing to read out; a nested record is descended into with a path instead), and `is_null` (the scalar holds loft's null, which is not `""`, `0` or `false`).

Two limits are worth knowing before reaching for it. Reflection does not WRITE: building or mutating a value by field name is a separate and larger question. And neither half works **inside a generic body**, which is parsed once against its type variable — `type_of(v)` there can only answer with that variable, and `field_value` is a compile error rather than an empty answer, since for its main caller an empty answer is a row with no columns. Call them where the concrete type is known.

## What it is

Bind a collection to a source and stop loading it. After `store_bind_lazy(parts, "inventory.store")` the collection is a window onto that source: a lookup that **misses** fetches exactly that one entry and keeps it, so the next lookup for the same key is an ordinary resident hit. There is no separate cache to manage — the collection *is* the working set.

Two kinds of source bind the same way:

- **A store IMAGE** — a local `.store` file, or an `http(s)://` URL served with Range. Whatever `store_load_key` accepts.
- **A DATABASE** — `sqlite:<path>`, where the entries are rows in a real relational table and the `SELECT` is **derived from the collection's own type**.

Binding is per **collection**, not per store, so `persons` and `companies` can come from different sources, and two collections of the same type can bind differently. Binding replaces, and may be done before the collection holds anything.

A lookup never raises, so a bare `null` cannot tell a key that is genuinely absent from a source you could not reach — two different facts, one stable and one not. Three calls separate them:

- `store_lazy_error(c)` — why the last fetch could not reach the source, or `""` when healthy. Reaching the source and not finding the key **clears** it, so a stale error never outlives the truth.
- `store_lazy_faults(c)` — how many fetches could not reach the source. `0` is healthy; after a traversal it answers "how incomplete am I".
- `store_lazy_clear(c)` — acknowledge those faults. It is the only thing that clears them: a later fetch that happens to succeed does not, because a traversal that missed data is still missing it.

## How it aids you

Work with a dataset far larger than memory without writing a loading step, a cache or an eviction policy. Point a collection at the source and use it like any other collection — the entries you touch are the entries you pay for, and the ones you touch twice are free the second time. Because the source can be a URL or a database, the same code queries a dataset sitting on static hosting or in a table other tools also use.

And when the data is incomplete, you find out. A traversal over an unreachable source cannot quietly answer "nothing there" — the fault count is sticky until you acknowledge it, so a partial answer cannot be mistaken for a complete one.

## Example

```loft
struct Part { const id: integer, name: text }

fn main() {
    // Stand in for a dataset you shipped earlier: a plain `.store` image.
    src: hash<Part[id]> = [];
    store_persist_bind(src, "inventory.store");
    src += Part { id: 7, name: "bolt" };
    src += Part { id: 91, name: "washer" };

    // Bind an EMPTY collection to that image. Nothing is loaded yet.
    parts: hash<Part[id]> = [];
    store_bind_lazy(parts, "inventory.store");
    println("resident: {parts.len()}");                 // resident: 0

    p = parts[91];                                      // a miss fetches this one entry
    println("{p.name}, resident: {parts.len()}");       // washer, resident: 1

    q = parts[91];                                      // a hit never leaves the process
    println("same: {p == q}, resident: {parts.len()}"); // same: true, resident: 1

    // A key the source does not have reads as null — and asking why tells a
    // genuine absence apart from a source you could not reach.
    missing = parts[404];
    println("missing: {missing == null}, faults: {store_lazy_faults(parts)}");

    delete("inventory.store");
    delete("inventory.store.dschema");
}
```

The source is **pinned when you bind**, so a traversal sees one consistent world; if the image changes underneath, the fetch is refused and reported through the fault channel rather than silently mixing two versions. Assigning `= []` to a bound collection reclaims what it holds while keeping the binding, which is the blunt way to cap a working set.

## Binding to a database

`store_bind_lazy(persons, "sqlite:people.db")` needs no query and no mapping. The collection's own type says everything: the table is the element type's name, the columns are its fields, and the `WHERE` is its key — so `hash<Person[id]>` over `struct Person { const id: integer, name: text }` fetches with

```sql
SELECT "id", "name" FROM "person" WHERE "id" = ?
```

The database owns the schema — loft binds to tables that already exist, writes nothing, and issues no DDL. Before it answers anything it checks once that the columns are there and that an **index** actually serves the lookup, because a missing index is not an error: it is a silent collapse from lazy to table-scan.

Two calls cover what a key cannot express. Both pull rows INTO the collection, so a record found either way is the SAME record a keyed lookup finds:

```loft
// One query, many rows — the cure for fetching 100 records one at a time.
got = store_lazy_range(events, 100, 199);

// A predicate the key cannot express. Written down, so the cost is visible.
found = store_lazy_query(persons, "name LIKE 'Ada%'");
```

Because the collection is the working set, `len(c)` is the RESIDENT count and iteration walks what has been touched — never "what exists in the table". A slice never fetches; ask for a range explicitly.

The companion feature is `store_load_key` and friends (#93), where you say explicitly which entries to fetch; this is the same machinery driven by the lookups themselves.

## What it is

`s#fields` walks a struct's scalar fields as CODE. It is syntax rather than a function: the loop is unrolled at compile time into one block per field, and `f` carries that field's `name`, its `nullable` flag, and a `value` whose payload is **already typed** — `FvBool` · `FvInt` · `FvLong` · `FvFloat` · `FvSingle` · `FvChar` · `FvText`.

## How it aids you

Printing a record, hashing one, building a query row, diffing two values field by field — written once, against any struct, with no position arithmetic. Because the payload arrives typed, what you write inside the loop is ordinary loft code the compiler checks for every field it generates, so a field whose type the loop does not handle is visible at compile time rather than as a missing line at run time.

## Example

```loft
struct Row { id: integer, name: text?, active: boolean }

fn main() {
    row = Row { id: 7, name: "ada", active: true };

    // One block per field, generated at compile time; `v` arrives already typed.
    for f in row#fields {
        if f.value is FvInt  { v } { println("{f.name}: integer = {v}") }
        if f.value is FvText { v } { println("{f.name}: text = {v}") }
        if f.value is FvBool { v } { println("{f.name}: boolean = {v}") }
    }
}
```

**Only scalar fields are visited.** A nested record, a vector or a keyed collection has no `FieldValue` payload and is skipped. `type_of(x).fields` reports those too, so the two lists agree on the scalars and reflection's is the longer one.

**`nullable` says a sentinel is possible.** The payload variants are typed non-null and `τ?` shares `τ`'s runtime layout, so a null field's value arrives inside `FvText` / `FvInt` / … as loft's null. The flag is what tells you that can happen; it mirrors `FieldInfo.nullable`.

**It is an unroll, so it costs code** — two work-refs per field per loop, live until scope exit. Collect several answers in ONE loop rather than writing three loops over the same struct.

**Which half of reflection to reach for.** `#fields` gives you per-field CODE with typed payloads and no runtime walk; `type_of` + `field_value` (#107) give you per-field DATA you can carry, index and compare. Use `#fields` to *write* something per field; use reflection when the field list itself is the value — when it has to line up with a table definition, a wire format, or another type's fields.

## What it is

`store_release(collection)` says *everything I have written so far is finished*: it starts writing that out to the bound file and drops it from the process's resident set, answering with the bytes dropped. `0` when there was nothing to drop, or the collection is not bound to a file.

Content is untouched and every reference into the collection stays valid — nothing moves and nothing is freed. Reading a released record simply re-reads it from the file, one page fault later.

## How it aids you

A generator streaming a large collection into a bound store holds everything it has written, and the kernel only works out which pages are finished by evicting some wrong ones first. Saying so directly turns that cliff into a curve. Measured on a 20 000-record build, one call per record: **peak memory 44.3 MB → 2.2 MB (20×), and the wall clock does not move.**

Because it is a hint, calling it too often — or on a collection that is not bound — costs a little speed and can never cost an answer.

## Example

```loft
struct Tile { const key: integer, const points: vector<integer> }

fn main() {
    // A generator streaming more data than you want resident, into a store bound
    // FIRST — the file is the arena, so what it writes lands in file-backed pages.
    tiles: hash<Tile[key]> = [];
    store_persist_bind(tiles, "tiles.store");

    dropped = 0;
    for k in 0..2000 {
        tiles += Tile { key: k, points: [] };
        t = tiles[k];
        for i in 0..40 { t.points += [k * 100 + i] }
        dropped += store_release(tiles);          // this tile is finished
    }
    println("held: {tiles.len()}, dropped from memory: {dropped > 0}");

    // Nothing about the data changed. A released record is read back from the
    // file — same value, one page fault later.
    println("tile 7 still reads {tiles[7].points[39]}");

    delete("tiles.store");
    delete("tiles.store.dschema");
}
```

**It pays when records are written in key order and not returned to.** A build that keeps many records open at once relocates its growing vectors and leaves the store scattered with free blocks — 3 691 against 10 for the same data written in order — and the allocator's own free-space tree lives *inside* those freed blocks, so it keeps re-reading exactly the region the release just dropped. The same call then gives back 1.0× instead of 20×. If your generator streams in cell order this is close to free; if it does not, this is a good reason to sort it first.

Not `store_reclaim()`, which hands back the file's unused **tail** and changes its length — this changes only what is resident and never the file's size. Not a durability barrier either: it asks for writeback to *start*, and `store_durable_seal()` is what promises the bytes have landed. Unix with a file-backed store; a no-op that answers `0` elsewhere.

## What it is

An interface can name a **companion type** that goes with the implementor, not
only a set of methods. A connection and the cursor it produces are one contract:
`type Rows: Cursor` says "and a row type, which must be a `Cursor`", and a
signature names it as `Self.Rows`.

An implementor writes no `impl` block — satisfaction stays structural — so the
companion is INFERRED from the implementor's own signature: where the interface
wrote `Self.Rows`, the implementor wrote a concrete type, and that is it.

## How it aids you

Without this a cursor has to become state ON the connection, so a connection can
hold only one and the type system cannot say so — a second `select` silently
replaces the first. With it the cursor is its own type, two of them coexist, and
the compiler checks the companion satisfies what the interface promised.

It costs nothing at run time: generics specialise per concrete type, so an
associated type is a compile-time name and the generated code is identical to the
same body written against the concrete types by hand.

## Example

```loft
interface Cursor {
    fn width(self: Self) -> integer
}

interface Source {
    type Rows: Cursor                   // the companion type
    fn open(self: Self) -> Self.Rows    // named in a signature as `Self.<Name>`
}

struct FileRows { w: integer }
struct FileSource { path: text }

fn width(self: FileRows) -> integer { return self.w; }
fn open(self: FileSource) -> FileRows { return FileRows { w: len(self.path) }; }

// The generic holds the companion and calls the bound's methods on it. Two of
// them coexist, which is what a cursor kept ON the connection could never do.
fn total_width<S: Source>(s: S) -> integer {
    a = s.open();
    b = s.open();
    return a.width() + b.width();
}

fn main() {
    println("{total_width(FileSource { path: "abcd" })}");   // 8
}
```

## Rules worth knowing

- **Every position must agree.** The companion is read from the return AND the
  parameters — an implementor whose `open` yields one type while its `feed` takes
  another does not have one companion, and is refused rather than resolved by
  declaration order.
- **The bound is checked per implementor**, and the message names all four
  parties, because three of them are invisible at the call site:
  *"'S1' binds 'Source.Rows' to 'Bad', which does not satisfy the declared bound
  'Cursor': missing width"*.
- **A bound-less `type Held` still binds.** Nothing of the companion's own is
  callable, but the interface that named it can take it back
  (`fn keep(self: Self, h: Self.Held)`).
- The name is a type name and is enforced CamelCase; `Self.<Name>` is spellable
  only inside the declaring interface's body.

## What it is

A type that defines `OpIndex` is subscripted like a built-in collection. `x[i]`
lowers to the ordinary method call `OpIndex(x, i)`, so a matrix, a bitset, a row
or a ring buffer reads as `x[i]` rather than `x.at(i)`.

The index type is whatever the method declares — there is no requirement that a
subscript be an integer, so a row addressed by column NAME takes a `text`.

## How it aids you

This was the last place a library type was visibly not a built-in one: every
other operator already had its `OpCamelCase` method, while a user struct met
*"Indexing a non vector"*. A wrapper type now reads the way its users expect,
and what a miss means is the TYPE's decision rather than the language's.

## Example

```loft
struct Ring {
    data: vector<integer>,
    start: integer,
}

// `x[i]` on a Ring is whatever this says it is.
fn OpIndex(self: Ring, i: integer) -> integer {
    n = len(self.data);
    if n == 0 { return 0; }
    return self.data[(self.start + i) % n] ?? 0;
}

fn main() {
    r = Ring { data: [10, 20, 30], start: 1 };
    println("{r[0]} {r[1]} {r[2]}");    // 20 30 10
}
```

## An interface can require it

```loft
interface Indexable {
    op [] (self: Self, i: integer) -> integer
    fn count(self: Self) -> integer
}

fn total<I: Indexable>(x: I) -> integer {
    s = 0;
    for i in 0..x.count() { s += x[i]; }
    return s;
}
```

Inside a generic only the BOUNDS may be relied on, so an unbounded `<I>` cannot
be subscripted even when every type it is used with defines `OpIndex`.

## `OpIndex` reads

`x[i] = …` is refused, and the message says why: a writing counterpart needs its
own method and a decision about whether `x[i] += 1` may read-modify-write. A type
that must be written through offers a setter — `x.set(i, v)`.
